## 5. Standard Page Structure & Layout Rules

To ensure HTML validity, clean SEO semantics, and structural consistency across the site:

### A. The Page Layout Flow
Every page template under `src/pages/` must strictly adhere to the following tag hierarchy:
```astro
<Layout title="..." description="...">
  <main class="page-wrapper">
    <section id="[unique-section-id]">
      <!-- Content -->
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
```

### B. Structural Requirements
1. **Single `<main>` Element**:
   - `src/layouts/Layout.astro` acts as the document layout shell and **must not** contain any `<main>` tags.
   - Individual page files are responsible for rendering their own single `<main class="page-wrapper">` element.
2. **Semantic Section Elements**:
   - All primary layout blocks inside `<main>` must be wrapped in `<section>` tags.
   - Every `<section>` tag must contain a descriptive, unique `id` attribute (e.g., `id="about-hero"`, `id="pricing-plans"`).
3. **Full-Bleed Page Overrides**:
   - For pages requiring edge-to-edge content (such as `index.astro` containing the `#hero`), use the standard `<main class="page-wrapper">` wrapper.
   - The top and horizontal paddings are automatically reset to `0` via the CSS selector `.page-wrapper:has(#hero)` inside `global.css`.
4. **No Client Directives on Astro Components**:
   - Never append client directives (e.g. `client:load`, `client:visible`) when importing or rendering static Astro components (e.g., `<FAQs />`). Astro components compile down to static HTML and cannot be hydrated in the client browser.
